        # a61f450fb/ka-0000000673 thaw-state force). HIGHEST precedence after the
        # thaw-state force — overrides the d-in-gofer AND the bsrc[d] branches below.
        # The two projected serviceaccount token mounts MUST bind to the controller-
        # owned warm copy ($BUNDLE/.gofer/<n>, relocated under /var/lib/gardenio and so
        # surviving the [4] placeholder force-delete), NEVER to bsrc[d] (Pod B's LIVE
        # projected dir under /var/lib/kubelet/pods/<podB>/...). bsrc[d] RESOLVES at the
        # [3a] guard (Pod B is alive until [4]) but [4] force-deletes Pod B BEFORE the
        # [5] restore, so the kubelet reclaims Pod B's projected volumes and the source
        # dir VANISHES — the restored sandbox then sees the serviceaccount DIRECTORY
        # itself ENOENT and coop fails closed with FreshnessError::Missing ("token file
        # not found"), NOT PodAIdentity (instrumented canary #7, prod 2026.180.27).
        # The pre-fix d-in-gofer branch already pinned KEYED token dirs to the warm copy,
        # but it was NOT robust: any path where the token dir is ABSENT from the gofer
        # manifest (a best-effort copytree skip at freeze, or a snapshot frozen by an
        # older controller before the ka-0000008074 sweep) fell THROUGH to bsrc[d] -> the
        # ephemeral [4]-teardown trap. Forcing it here, and refusing to fall through,
        # closes that gap EXACTLY as a61f450fb forced the thaw-state source instead of
        # trusting bsrc[d]. vfs.CompleteRestore re-walks only the mountpoint (proven by
        # canary #7 restoring rc=0 against a re-pointed thaw-state mount), so binding the
        # token mount to the warm copy is restore-safe.
        if d in gofer:
            _warm=bundle+"/.gofer/"+gofer[d]
            m["source"]=_warm
